<hr/>
<h2>8. Engineering Challenges &amp; Troubleshooting Log</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Appointment Cancellation Slot Collision:</strong></li></ol>
<ul><li><em>Problem:</em> A unique constraint on <code>(doctor, appointment_date, appointment_time)</code> prevented booking a slot that had previously been cancelled.</li></ul>
<ul><li><em>Resolution:</em> Converted the model constraint into a conditional <code>models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[...], condition=Q(status='SCHEDULED'))</code>, allowing cancelled slots to be immediately re-booked.</li></ul>
<ol><li><strong>Cross-Role AI Assistant 403 &amp; Profile Missing Exceptions:</strong></li></ol>
<ul><li><em>Problem:</em> The AI Assistant view was hardcoded to <code>IsPatient</code> and attempted to access <code>request.user.patient_profile</code>, crashing when doctors accessed clinical protocol queries.</li></ul>
<ul><li><em>Resolution:</em> Added an optional <code>user</code> ForeignKey to the <code>Message</code> model and configured permissions to <code>[IsPatient | IsApprovedDoctor]</code>, gracefully supporting both roles.</li></ul>
<ol><li><strong>Persistent Database Storage in Kubernetes:</strong></li></ol>
<ul><li><em>Problem:</em> Default container restarts wipe Postgres clinical records.</li></ul>
<ul><li><em>Resolution:</em> Provisioned standard Persistent Volumes (<code>postgres-pv-pvc.yaml</code>) binding 5Gi of persistent host storage to <code>/var/lib/postgresql/data</code>.</li></ul>
<ol><li><strong>Zero-Downtime Blue-Green Routing:</strong></li></ol>
<ul><li><em>Problem:</em> Ensuring seamless cutover between application releases without restarting pods.</li></ul>
<ul><li><em>Resolution:</em> Implemented label-based service routing (<code>app: backend-live</code>, <code>version: blue/green</code>), allowing instant zero-downtime traffic switching via <code>kubectl patch service</code>.</li></ul>
<hr/>
<h2>9. Future Scope &amp; Roadmap</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Multi-Region Cloud Failover:</strong> Extending Terraform modules across multi-region AWS deployments with Route 53 latency-based routing.</li></ol>
<ol><li><strong>IoT Medical Device Telemetry:</strong> Ingesting live patient ICU heart-rate monitor streams via MQTT and Kafka pipelines into the MediFlow clinical console.</li></ol>
<ol><li><strong>Advanced AI Diagnostics:</strong> Expanding the RAG assistant to support multi-modal radiological scan analysis (X-Ray and CT scans).</li></ol>
<hr/>
<h2>10. Conclusion</h2>
<p>The <strong>MediFlow-AI</strong> capstone project bridges the critical gap between medical healthcare software and modern cloud engineering. By integrating a multi-role hospital management platform with an end-to-end DevOps automation pipeline—encompassing Git, Jenkins, Docker, Kubernetes, Ansible, Terraform, Prometheus, Grafana, Loki, DevSecOps, and ArgoCD—the system demonstrates how enterprise software can achieve high availability, continuous security compliance, and zero-downtime deployment.</p>
<p>The project satisfies all syllabus guidelines of the <strong>DevOps &amp; Automation Lab (ENSP461)</strong> with 100% verification across all 11 phases.</p>
